ndividual  accessions would be applied per genome  so if you had two isolates of E coli  from to pay  in a hospital we would be providing  separate accession numbers for both of  those so there was no way to correlate  easily that the sequences were actually  identical at the protein level and we  were having a huge increase in  redundancy in the refseq data set and so  we undertook a massive shift in our data  model to address the redundancy issue we  went through an intermediate stage where  we had our original accession numbers  cross linking to our new truly non  redundant refseq protein data set and  these currently help an accession number  prefix that begins with a W key so if  you see an accession WP followed by  underscore you know you're looking at a  truly non redundant refseq protein  record our current model which is the  completion of our transition in our  protein management is that all of our  bacterial and your archaeal records are  directly annotated with the  non-redundant protein record so what  this means is for a given CD s let's say  the first one on my on my picture here  if this protein is identically found in  five other genomes from the same or  different species then we annotate all  six of those genomes with the exact same  accession number so you'll there will be  six genomes that are providing cross  references to this identical protein  sequence so we've significantly reduced  the redundancy in our protein data set  you can see some of the actual measures  of that redundancy in our documentation  page the link is shown at the bottom of  this page where you just plug in the  ncbi root URL here so this offers us an  advantage in terms of redundancy  management but it also has had a very  positive secondary benefit and giving us  a really good way to manage protein  names we can now manage protein names  directly on this level of non-redundant  protein records  without having to Rihanna Tate the  genome we can update the protein record  