own here to  access this page this is a very useful  page it tells you how many genomes a  given protein record was annotated on in  this case I box 3 so this same protein  accession number has been annotated on  three different reps each genomes the  accessions are shown here and the  coordinates of the annotation are shown  here the organism information is shown  in the column to the right and so you  can see that these are three very  different bacterial organisms and the  lowest common denominator for these is  at the bacteria level in some cases this  may be an indicator that there was a  problem in identifying the correct  organism for a submission but in other  cases this is really reflecting very  interesting biology and last but not  least in the process of converting to  our new data model there was an awful  lot of suppression that happened we  suppress the intermediate level of  records the Y P and NP accession numbers  on all of our representative invariant  genomes I just want to make a note that  for the reference genomes they continue  to use the data model where there is an  independent accession defined for those  for the reference genomes for those  records that were suppressed we have  provided a custom message on those  records so if you go to this URL you  will see a very important message  provided that tells you that this  accession that has been suppressed has  indeed been replaced by and I get an on  redundant protein record then you can  navigate to that protein record to the  identical protein report or a  documentation about our me annotation  project and last there is a  comprehensive mapping file provided for  those who would like to have a  comprehensive report by FTP this file is  available in the refseq release catalog  directory this was put out in the  beginning of this month in july when we  process our next refseq release this  file will be moved into the release  catalog archive directory will where it  will continue to be available this file  provides mapping that  the old suppressed protein accession  number to the alive non-redundant  protein accession that is replacing it  it also mapped old locus tags - new  locus tags we had to annotate our  genomes with new locus tags because we  are now providing de novo genome  annotation for all of our records and  there are some new genes that are being  called so we cannot rely on the existing  locus tags that may have been submitted  it also reports information on gene ID  mapping for people who are interested in  that information commencing as gene  resource and I would just like to thank  people who have done all of the work on  these changes if you have questions you  can look at the online documentation  that my provided links to in these  slides you can also look at our FAQ or  you can contact us and either info at  NCBI or webinars at NCBI to follow up  with questions thank you so the question  is what is the source of the annotations  qualifiers attached to the non-redundant  protein entry and what is the procedure  if I have a question about that  annotation so are you talking about I'm  not sure exactly what you mean by  qualifiers you're talking about the  organism information the protein name or  something else oh the the product so  you're talking about the protein name so  initially the source was what came in  from GenBank NCBI has been working on  protein clustering and naming things  that are similar by homology for a  couple of years in the beginning of  March we created this new name  management database that really gives us  a lot of power to improve our protein  names and we have already improved about  1.2 million protein names and this is a  an area that we are extremely actively  working on so if you have specific name  examples then yes please send them if ik  names that are bad then yes please send  that information to us we're very very  interested in improving the quality of  the names on our non-redundant protein  